  <section class="sec">
    <h2>My view</h2>
    <p class="p">The binding constraint on Mexican growth is no longer capital, it is connection. There is money willing to build here and there are sites that cannot get power, water or a rail slot on the timeline the investment needs. That is a slower problem than a tariff round and it gets far less attention, which is precisely why I think it deserves a standing section.</p>
    <p class="p">I read Pemex mainly as a fiscal story rather than an energy one. What it produces matters less to the rest of the economy than what it costs to keep solvent, because that cost competes directly with the transmission and water infrastructure the industrial build-out actually needs.</p>
    <p class="p change-mind"><b>What would change my mind:</b> transmission capacity and interconnection approvals rising fast enough that industrial projects stop citing power and water as their gating item. If the constraint stops binding, this section should be about cost rather than capacity.</p>
  </section>

